{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. One test assertion could be tighter, but I don't count that as a gap.\n\n**Implementation** (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, `AssessmentSources::prepare`, annotated `{impl}requirements.examples.authoring_inputs`):\n- `prepare` looks the subject up in the loaded definitions. It accepts any non-term kind, so named examples are covered as well as requirements.\n- It seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`, so the subject's own declaration is always the first mandatory input.\n- `source_input` resolves that selector through `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs`. For `Requirements` selectors this uses the declaration's raw lines. The mandatory input shown for this subject starts at `:::{req} authoring_inputs` and ends at `:::`, which confirms the text is the complete fenced declaration.\n- That input stays in the mandatory set. `generated_evidence_selector` never omits `Requirements` selectors, and the final set is deduplicated.\n- An unknown subject fails with an error before inference.\n\n**Tests** (`bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors`, annotated `{test}requirements.examples.authoring_inputs`):\n- The test declares a named example `case` (`:::{example} case ... :::`) in a project fixture.\n- It calls `AssessmentSources::prepare(root, \"requirements.case\")`.\n- It asserts that the subject kind is `Definition(Example)`.\n- It asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's own declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- It also asserts that preparing `requirements.ready` pulls in `requirements.case` through the cite relationship.\n\n**Minor weakness:** the example-subject assertion checks the selector name, not the literal fenced text. The shared `Requirements` resolution path returns the full declaration lines, and that behaviour is covered by the other input tests. The scenario and expected outcome are therefore adequately covered.",
